T* () Tj
T* (Machiavelli, in his ?History of Florence,? gives us a picture of the) Tj
T* (young men among whom his youth was passed. He writes: ?They were freer) Tj
T* (than their forefathers in dress and living, and spent more in other) Tj
T* (kinds of excesses, consuming their time and money in idleness, gaming,) Tj
T* (and women; their chief aim was to appear well dressed and to speak with) Tj
T* (wit and acuteness, whilst he who could wound others the most cleverly) Tj
T* (was thought the wisest.? In a letter to his son Guido, Machiavelli) Tj
T* (shows why youth should avail itself of its opportunities for study, and) Tj
T* (leads us to infer that his own youth had been so occupied. He writes:) Tj
T* (?I have received your letter, which has given me the greatest pleasure,) Tj
T* (especially because you tell me you are quite restored in health, than) Tj
T* (which I could have no better news; for if God grant life to you, and to) Tj
T* (me, I hope to make a good man of you if you are willing to do your) Tj
T* (share.? Then, writing of a new patron, he continues: ?This will turn) Tj
T* (out well for you, but it is necessary for you to study; since, then,) Tj
T* (you have no longer the excuse of illness, take pains to study letters) Tj
T* (and music, for you see what honour is done to me for the little skill I) Tj
T* (have. Therefore, my son, if you wish to please me, and to bring success) Tj
T* (and honour to yourself, do right and study, because others will help) Tj
