T* (?If I could have believed that Fortune would have cut me off in the) Tj
T* (midst of the career which was leading to that glory which all my) Tj
T* (successes promised, I should have laboured less, and I should have left) Tj
T* (thee, if a smaller state, at least with fewer enemies and perils,) Tj
T* (because I should have been content with the governorships of Lucca and) Tj
T* (Pisa. I should neither have subjugated the Pistoians, nor outraged the) Tj
T* (Florentines with so many injuries. But I would have made both these) Tj
T* (peoples my friends, and I should have lived, if no longer, at least) Tj
T* (more peacefully, and have left you a state without a doubt smaller, but) Tj
T* (one more secure and established on a surer foundation. But Fortune, who) Tj
T* (insists upon having the arbitrament of human affairs, did not endow me) Tj
T* (with sufficient judgment to recognize this from the first, nor the time) Tj
T* (to surmount it. Thou hast heard, for many have told thee, and I have) Tj
T* (never concealed it, how I entered the house of thy father whilst yet a) Tj
T* (boy?a stranger to all those ambitions which every generous soul should) Tj
T* (feel?and how I was brought up by him, and loved as though I had been) Tj
T* (born of his blood; how under his governance I learned to be valiant and) Tj
T* (capable of availing myself of all that fortune, of which thou hast been) Tj
T* (witness. When thy good father came to die, he committed thee and all) Tj
T* (his possessions to my care, and I have brought thee up with that love,) Tj
T* (and increased thy estate with that care, which I was bound to show. And) Tj
T* (in order that thou shouldst not only possess the estate which thy) Tj
T* (father left, but also that which my fortune and abilities have gained,) Tj
T* (I have never married, so that the love of children should never deflect) Tj
T* (my mind from that gratitude which I owed to the children of thy father.) Tj
T* (Thus I leave thee a vast estate, of which I am well content, but I am) Tj
T* (deeply concerned, inasmuch as I leave it thee unsettled and insecure.) Tj
T* (Thou hast the city of Lucca on thy hands, which will never rest) Tj
T* (contented under thy government. Thou hast also Pisa, where the men are) Tj
T* (of nature changeable and unreliable, who, although they may be) Tj
T* (sometimes held in subjection, yet they will ever disdain to serve under) Tj
T* (a Lucchese. Pistoia is also disloyal to thee, she being eaten up with) Tj
T* (factions and deeply incensed against thy family by reason of the wrongs) Tj
T* (recently inflicted upon them. Thou hast for neighbours the offended) Tj
T* (Florentines, injured by us in a thousand ways, but not utterly) Tj
T* (destroyed, who will hail the news of my death with more delight than) Tj
T* (they would the acquisition of all Tuscany. In the Emperor and in the) Tj
T* (princes of Milan thou canst place no reliance, for they are far) Tj
T* (distant, slow, and their help is very long in coming. Therefore, thou) Tj
T* (hast no hope in anything but in thine own abilities, and in the memory) Tj
T* (of my valour, and in the prestige which this latest victory has brought) Tj
T* (thee; which, as thou knowest how to use it with prudence, will assist) Tj
